/// A join inside a loop, with one arm that hands out a name bound outside it.
/// Two rules meet here and neither is wrong on its own: an arm that yields an
/// outer name stands that name down, because the join's result is what
/// releases the value now; and a `let` owns what the core lowered into it, so
/// `picked` is released at the end of the block. The block is the loop body.
///
/// The first turn frees the array `names` holds. The second turn reads it and
/// frees it again. Both engines lowered it through the same core; the native
/// binary said `free audit: double or foreign free` and exited 134 under
/// `VYRN_LEAK_CHECK=1`, and the wasm engine, whose allocator audits nothing,
/// printed `5` and said nothing. The kernel refuses it now, and the menu says
/// `.copy()`.
///
/// The same shape at a `match` arm is the same refusal. A REBIND is not: `xs =
/// if c { xs } else { .. }` binds nothing, so nothing is released per turn.
fn main() -> Int64 {
    let names: Array<String> = ["a", "b"]
    let mut n = 0
    let mut i = 0
    while i < 3 {
        let picked = if i > 0 { names } else { ["z"] }
        n = n + picked.length
        i = i + 1
    }
    print("\{n}")
    return 0
}
